{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"alias":"tearful-cleansable-gopher","creation_channel":"cli","description":"## Context\n\nrebar's plan vocabulary carries two headings for one concept. `## Acceptance Criteria` is the\nuniversal blocking floor: P1 (`det_floor.py`) and the standalone `check-ac` gate require a\n`- [ ]` checklist with >=1 item on EVERY ticket type. `## Success Criteria` is a second heading\nthat the author guide asks container/epic tickets to add on top.\n\nInvestigation showed the split is vestigial vocabulary, not a live semantic axis. Every\ncriterion that touches them POOLS them and applies ONE bar:\n\n- F1 opens \"Examine each acceptance/success criterion for measurability and whether an agent\n  can complete it within ONE working session\", and its routing is\n  `applies_at: {\"suppress_types\": [\"bug\"]}` — no scope restriction, so it binds containers\n  exactly as it binds leaves. There is no SC carve-out.\n- G3's checklist reads \"The union of children covers each parent acceptance/success criterion\".\n- G7 fetches the parent's \"What/Scope/Success Criteria/Acceptance Criteria\" as one contract.\n- The completion verifier lists both as completion requirements.\n\nNot one criterion applies a different bar to SC. Altitude is already carried by ROUTING — G3 is\n`applies_at: {\"scope\": [\"container\"]}`, firing on whether a ticket has children — so the heading\nname carries no load. The only divergence is the author guide's prose, which implies a split the\ngates never implemented.\n\nThe split is also actively harmful. `_count_ac_items` counts `- [ ]` items ONLY under\n`## Acceptance Criteria`, resetting at the next `##`. SC is conventionally written as plain\nbullets, so criteria living under SC (a) do not count toward the P1 blocking floor, (b) cannot\ncarry the `[operator-attested]` tag — the completion verifier matches that token only on\ncheckbox text, so an SC item is silently judged by the codebase bar — and (c) are not\nindividually checkable. Collapsing the vocabulary therefore TIGHTENS the gate.\n\n## Why now\n\nRenaming criteria text rotates the `regver` criteria-registry stamp. Until ticket\n`1f32-f86e-8a51-4d8e` that invalidated every outstanding plan-review attestation repo-wide\n(632 tickets, ~40 open/in_progress), which made this migration prohibitively expensive. ADR 0053\ngrandfathers registry drift at the claim gate, so the rename is now affordable.\n\n## Scope\n\nCollapse `## Success Criteria` into `## Acceptance Criteria` across the live vocabulary:\ndetector regexes, reviewer prompts, the criteria routing index, author-facing guides, the\ngenerated criteria guide, eval specs and gold-set expectations, the test corpus, and the 108\ntickets in the store that carry the old heading.\n\nSequenced expand-contract: detectors accept BOTH headings first, the vocabulary and data move\nunder that umbrella, and the alias is removed last. No window in which a ticket is\nun-reviewable because its heading is in transit.\n\nIN SCOPE: the `## Success Criteria` -> `## Acceptance Criteria` collapse and the SC -> AC\nvocabulary in prompts/docs/tests, plus migrating store tickets' SC bullets to AC checkboxes.\n\nOUT OF SCOPE:\n- The `dod_uncertifiable` / \"definition-of-done\" vocabulary in the verifier prompts (related but\n  a separate concept; may be picked up as discovered work).\n- Removing `clarity-check` (independently planned for removal; this epic only stops it keying\n  on the SC heading).\n- Frozen test fixtures that RECORD historical text (`tests/fixtures/jira/*`) and historical\n  citations to a past ticket's numbered criterion.\n- Anything matching `O-RAN-SC` (a Linux Foundation telecom consortium) in ADRs 0021/0022/0023\n  and `.github/workflows/gerrit-verify.yaml`, and the minified vendor bundle\n  `llm/workflow/editor_assets/dist/editor.js`.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [ ] No live source file under `src/rebar/` matches `Success Criteria` / `success criteria` /\n      `success criterion` (case-insensitive), verified by a repo grep that excludes the frozen\n      fixtures and vendor bundle named in Scope.\n- [ ] Both bare-`SC` tokens meaning Success Criteria — `plan_review_G3.md:12`\n      (\"THREE SC-CONTRADICTION PATTERNS\" and \"'SC says zero matches...'\") — read AC, and the\n      four `O-RAN-SC` ADR/workflow occurrences are byte-identical to their pre-migration state.\n- [ ] A ticket whose plan uses only `## Acceptance Criteria` passes plan review at container\n      level: G3's child-coverage audit runs against the parent's AC with no criteria lost.\n- [ ] The 108 store tickets carrying `## Success Criteria` are migrated so their criteria sit\n      under `## Acceptance Criteria` as `- [ ]` checklist items and are counted by\n      `_count_ac_items`.\n- [ ] `docs/plan-review-criteria-guide.md` is REGENERATED (not hand-edited) from the updated\n      registry and contains no SC vocabulary.\n- [ ] `make lint`, `make typecheck` and the full pytest suite pass, and every landed change\n      carries `Verified +1`.","id":"1386-5e7c-391d-4a74","parent_id":"","priority":2,"tags":["vocabulary","plan-review","migration"],"ticket_type":"epic","title":"Collapse Success Criteria into Acceptance Criteria across rebar's plan vocabulary"},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"CREATE","timestamp":1784944563136270001,"uuid":"7a701473-8736-41ed-9d03-b4c7e1dc2394"}